{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_bgxi6hrqtsgx6brsyadfrh6h7nwrwygy2i5fgrnloucs72fuivpa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Verification complete — all gates pass.** 92 tests, 92 passing, across every build configuration.\n\n## Two real bugs found and fixed\n\n**1. The test harness was verifying a stale `.so` (critical)**\n`cargo test` builds the test binaries but never rebuilds a `cdylib` — nothing links against it. The suite kept `dlopen`ing an artifact from an earlier `cargo build`. I proved this by mutation testing: **0 of 28** deliberate breakages of the Rust source were detected. Every test \"passed\" while comparing against a library that didn't match its own source. Fixed by having the harness invoke `cargo build --release` itself and then *assert* the `.so` is newer than every `src/*.rs`. Detection went to **27/28**; the survivor (`%ld`→`%d` printing a value that's always `6`, same 64-bit register on x86-64 SysV) is a provably equivalent mutant.\n\n**2. `&mut *ptr` on a caller-supplied pointer diverged from C (translation fix)**\nThe three functions taking `struct ConfigFlags *` used `&mut *flags` to reach the bit-fields. C callers may pass any pointer; forming a Rust reference attaches a guarantee the input doesn't carry. In the **debug** profile this aborted with **SIGABRT** where C takes **SIGSEGV**. Replaced with raw-pointer read/write accessors that never form a reference. Caught only because I cross-checked the debug cdylib and tested the null-pointer rows *for real* (forked child, comparing termination signals) instead of writing them off as \"crashes both\".\n\nA third issue was harness-only: fd redirection is process-global, so libtest's own progress output leaked into captured stdout. Pinned `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` in `.cargo/config.toml`.\n\n## Coverage\nEvery call compares the return value **plus** stdout bytes **plus** stderr bytes, and all 4 bytes of any `ConfigFlags` passed. Exhaustive where feasible: all 256 flag bit patterns, the full 9⁴ int-boundary cross-product for `envy`, the full 675-combination environment cross-product, and a caller-composed low-level pipeline reaching states (`cache_enabled=0`, `log_level≠3`, `reserved=1`) that `envy` itself can never produce. Also cross-checked against gcc at `-O2`, which matters because the C relies on signed-overflow and negative-shift behaviour that is formally UB.\n\nI confirmed gcc's bit-field codegen via `objdump` (byte-0-only read-modify-write, padding untouched) rather than assuming the layout, and left the C's surprising behaviours intact — `PROG_OPTIMIZE=0` *enables* optimize, `PROG_VERBOSE=true` is false while `xx1xx` is true, an empty env var returns `0` not the default, and a negative result discards all arithmetic to return `param1`.\n\nOne judgement call worth flagging: `translation/Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]`, so the \"every feature combination\" gate reduces to one combination. I ran `--no-default-features` as well and made `run_all.sh` enumerate subsets automatically, so the gate stays honest if features are ever added. Likewise `c50_no_driver_binary_is_built` will fail if either build starts producing an executable, since the binary-stdout comparison is currently N/A.\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified; the `-O2` cross-check build goes to `translation/target/c_O2/`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":390,"cache_read_input_tokens":175554,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":390,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cebbbf64-74af-4f62-b3b1-18702bd3fc55","uuid":"1728eb90-04cb-4c60-be47-92573cf2ce94","timestamp":"2026-09-05T14:27:30.867Z"}
